## Why This Is Asked

Explores a novel variation of LPS with deletions, forcing a 3D DP and careful reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP: i, j bounds plus remaining deletions t.
- Transitions handle match, deletion of either end, and base cases.
- Reconstruction by tracing choices from dp[0][n-1][k].
- Space optimization via rolling t or two 2D layers.

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxLPSWithDeletions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][i][0] = 1;
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len - 1 < n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (s[i] === s[j]) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], (i+1<=j-1? dp[i+1][j-1][t] : 0) + 2);
        }
        if (t > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i+1][j][t-1], dp[i][j-1][t-1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0][n-1][k] || 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to O(nk) space while preserving O(n^2 k) time?
- How does the solution change if deletions must be exactly k?

## Why This Is Asked
Explores a new DP angle: subarray-level min as value, exact k non-overlapping blocks, with clear reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with exact block count
- Precomputation of subarray minima
- Backtracking to obtain endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
// Assumes A is 1-indexed, n = A.length-1, k given
function maxSumMinSubarrays(A, n, k) {
  const M = Array.from({length: n+2}, ()=>Array(n+2).fill(0));
  for (let l=1; l<=n; l++) {
    let m = A[l];
    for (let r=l; r<=n; r++) {
      m = Math.min(m, A[r]);
      M[l][r] = m;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const opt = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
    for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
      for (let l=1; l<=i; l++) {
        const cand = dp[l-1][t-1] + M[l][i];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; opt[i][t] = l; }
      }
    }
  }
  // backtrack for endpoints
  let i = n, t = k, blocks = [];
  while (t > 0) {
    const l = opt[i][t];
    blocks.push([l, i]);
    i = l-1; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return {sum: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with a 3D state on a grid path, a natural but less common extension of standard grid DP.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP on a grid, path constraints (only right/down), exact boosts
- Bottom-up iteration, reconstruction pointers, space/time considerations

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxBoostOnGrid(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array.from({ length: m }, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        const fromTop = i > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t] : NEG;
        const fromLeft = j > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t] : NEG;
        let best = Math.max(fromTop, fromLeft);
        if (best > NEG/2) dp[i][j][t] = best + grid[i][j];
        if (t > 0) {
          const hotTop = i > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t-1] : NEG;
          const hotLeft = j > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t-1] : NEG;
          const bestPrev = Math.max(hotTop, hotLeft);
          if (bestPrev > NEG/2) {
            dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], bestPrev + 2*grid[i][j]);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n-1][m-1][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize space if k is large but grid values are small?
- How would you reconstruct the boosted cell coordinates?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a beginner-friendly DP pattern with non-adjacent selections and reconstruction. It also shows handling multiple dimensions (i and t) and a simple heuristic constraint (no two boosts on consecutive days).

## Key Concepts
- DP with two states per index for constrained selections
- Reconstruction of chosen days
- Time/space: O(nk); note can optimize to O(k) space with rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxWorkload(workload, k) {
  const n = workload.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const noBoost = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const boost = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  noBoost[0][0] = workload[0];
  if (k >= 1) boost[0][1] = 2 * workload[0];
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      noBoost[i][t] = Math.max(noBoost[i-1][t], boost[i-1][t]) + workload[i];
    }
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      if (noBoost[i-1][t-1] > NEG) boost[i][t] = noBoost[i-1][t-1] + 2*workload[i];
    }
  }
  let best = NEG, bestT = 0, bestS = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
    if (noBoost[n-1][t] > best) { best = noBoost[n-1][t]; bestT = t; bestS = 0; }
    if (boost[n-1][t] > best) { best = boost[n-1][t]; bestT = t; bestS = 1; }
  }
  // reconstruction would backtrack using saved states (omitted for brevity)
  return { maxValue: best, boostedIndices: [] };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
